5. REORGANIZE CHARACTER DEFINITIONS

Existing issue

js/data/characters.js currently contains both fighter definitions along with their animation metadata, tuning, moves, and supporting helpers.

The file is over 60 KB and will continue growing as characters are introduced.

The project is intended to support many more than two characters, so this arrangement does not scale well.

Required improvement

Separate character-specific definitions into individual modules.

Suggested layout:

js/data/
├── characters.js
└── characters/
    ├── 0001.js
    ├── 0002.js
    └── helpers.js

Each character module should own that fighter’s specific information:

* Identifier and roster position.
* Availability.
* Display name.
* Animation definitions.
* Sprite metadata.
* Rendering proportions and anchors.
* Powers.
* Movement characteristics.
* Hitboxes and hurtboxes.
* Energy configuration.
* Defensive behavior.
* Attack definitions.
* Projectile definitions.
* Summon definitions.
* Techniques.
* Ability names.
* Mobile button artwork and metadata.

A fighter’s character-specific constants should generally live beside its own definition.

For example, constants for 0001’s attack animation rates do not belong in a global module unless they are genuinely shared configuration.

The same applies to 0002’s animation measurements, spinning-ball hitbox, and attack timing.

Keep character access stable

Preserve the existing public interface of js/data/characters.js where possible:

* CHARACTERS
* getCharacter
* isPlayable
* getPlayableCharacter
* playableCharacters
* characterFramePaths
* framePath
* frames

The existing module can become a small registry and compatibility entry point that imports individual character definitions.

This is particularly important because current tests register temporary fighters by modifying the exported CHARACTERS array.

Do not accidentally break that workflow by freezing the array, replacing it with a different instance, or changing its mutability.

Keep validation through assertLoadout.

Character asset organization

Preserve the established path convention:

assets/characters/<id>/<id>_<codename>_<frame>.png

Do not rename the current sprite assets for organizational aesthetics.

Importantly, preserve the established animation codename mouvment, including its spelling.

Although the word is unconventional, it is part of the existing canonical asset and animation naming contract in codename_rule.

Do not silently change it to movement.

The character definition’s movement configuration and the animation codename mouvment are separate concepts.

